Re-registering :rf.http/managed with :carriers through rf/reg-fx is the spelling spec 014 prescribes (spec/014-HTTPRequests.md ~1268 and ~1296). It also appears in docs/api/re-frame.http.md:210, docs/core how-to keep-secrets-out-of-traces.md:150,159, and docs/async/http-going-further.md:76-83. When called from an app namespace, it collides with the framework's own registration during image assembly. After that, EVERY default-image (rf/make-frame {}) throws :rf.error/image-duplicate-id, because the image sees [:fx :rf.http/managed] from two source namespaces: {:ns nil} from the framework, plus the app's namespace.

Measured on the JVM at trunk f89e28ec64 by the docs/async correctness review (rf2-s0i3z step 2), in a three-variant probe:
- no re-registration: make-frame OK, exit 0;
- the prescribed rf/reg-fx (macro, carries provenance): :rf.error/image-duplicate-id, exit 3;
- the fn form re-frame.fx/reg-fx (no provenance): make-frame OK, exit 0.

So the documented way to declare HTTP carriers breaks every later frame in the app. The owner decides the fix. Either image assembly treats an app re-registration of a framework fx as an override rather than a duplicate, or the spec and docs prescribe a different carriers mechanism, updating all four doc sites.

